  "question": {
    "text": "A rod, of length <math><mi>L</mi></math> at room temperature and uniform area of cross section <math><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo></math> is made of a metal having coefficient of linear expansion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>/</mo><mo>&#176;</mo><mi>C</mi></math> It is observed that an external compressive force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> is applied on each of its ends, prevents any change in the length of the rod when its temperature rises by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#160;</mo><mn>K</mn></math> Young&#39;s modulus, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math> for this metal is:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>&#945;</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>273</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac></math>",
